    "['ppp authentication [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `ppp authentication chap` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options authentication chap\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. You first need to set the PPP encapsulation on the interface\\n2. Then configure CHAP authentication under the PPP options\\n3. The authentication parameters (username/password) would be configured separately in Juniper's access profile configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options authentication chap', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration for the Cisco `ppp authentication chap` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options authentication chap\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. You first need to set the PPP encapsulation on the interface\\n2. Then configure CHAP authentication under the PPP options\\n3. The authentication parameters (username/password) would be configured separately in Juniper's access profile configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options authentication chap'}]]": 1,
    "['ppp chap hostname [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name rtb\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Junos, you first need to set the PPP encapsulation on the interface\\n- The equivalent of \"ppp chap hostname\" in Junos is \"ppp-options chap local-name\"\\n- The command sets the local CHAP hostname to \"rtb\" (equivalent to the Cisco configuration)\\n- Note that Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (se-1/0/0 instead of Serial1/0/0)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name rtb', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name rtb\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Junos, you first need to set the PPP encapsulation on the interface\\n- The equivalent of \"ppp chap hostname\" in Junos is \"ppp-options chap local-name\"\\n- The command sets the local CHAP hostname to \"rtb\" (equivalent to the Cisco configuration)\\n- Note that Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (se-1/0/0 instead of Serial1/0/0)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name rtb'}]]": 1,
    "['ppp chap password [encryption_type] [password]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the specified Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name \"rtb\"\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap secret \"$9$hq4S.3Z9Kv1Xe$dQ1VUJ4wCgWbR7yT2MnHx0O5PfL8A6EIkNlBsDaGc\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct equivalent for type-7 encrypted passwords (the \"7\" in Cisco\\'s command), so I\\'ve used the type-9 hashed secret from the username configuration which is more secure\\n2. The CHAP configuration is split into two parts in Juniper - the local-name (hostname) and the secret\\n3. The interface naming convention changes from Cisco\\'s \"Serial1/0/0\" to Juniper\\'s \"se-1/0/0\" format', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name \"rtb\"', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the specified Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name \"rtb\"\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap secret \"$9$hq4S.3Z9Kv1Xe$dQ1VUJ4wCgWbR7yT2MnHx0O5PfL8A6EIkNlBsDaGc\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct equivalent for type-7 encrypted passwords (the \"7\" in Cisco\\'s command), so I\\'ve used the type-9 hashed secret from the username configuration which is more secure\\n2. The CHAP configuration is split into two parts in Juniper - the local-name (hostname) and the secret\\n3. The interface naming convention changes from Cisco\\'s \"Serial1/0/0\" to Juniper\\'s \"se-1/0/0\" format', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name \"rtb\"'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap secret \"$9$hq4S.3Z9Kv1Xe$dQ1VUJ4wCgWbR7yT2MnHx0O5PfL8A6EIkNlBsDaGc\"', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration for the specified Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 encapsulation ppp\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap local-name \"rtb\"\\nset interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap secret \"$9$hq4S.3Z9Kv1Xe$dQ1VUJ4wCgWbR7yT2MnHx0O5PfL8A6EIkNlBsDaGc\"\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct equivalent for type-7 encrypted passwords (the \"7\" in Cisco\\'s command), so I\\'ve used the type-9 hashed secret from the username configuration which is more secure\\n2. The CHAP configuration is split into two parts in Juniper - the local-name (hostname) and the secret\\n3. The interface naming convention changes from Cisco\\'s \"Serial1/0/0\" to Juniper\\'s \"se-1/0/0\" format', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/0 ppp-options chap secret \"$9$hq4S.3Z9Kv1Xe$dQ1VUJ4wCgWbR7yT2MnHx0O5PfL8A6EIkNlBsDaGc\"'}]]": 1,
