    "['aaa new-model', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset system authentication-order password\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco `aaa new-model` command enables AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) services on the device. In Juniper, the closest equivalent is to set the authentication order to use local passwords, which is done with `set system authentication-order password`. This configures the device to use locally configured passwords for authentication, similar to how AAA with local authentication works in Cisco.', 'trans_template': 'set system authentication-order password'}]]": 1,
    "['aaa authentication ppp default local', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system authentication-order password', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system authentication-order password', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset system authentication-order password\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco command `aaa authentication ppp default local` configures the device to use local authentication for PPP connections. In Juniper, the equivalent is to set the authentication order to use local passwords via `set system authentication-order password`. This tells the Juniper device to consult its local user database for authentication.\\n\\nNote: While this is the functional equivalent, Juniper handles PPP authentication slightly differently than Cisco. You might also need additional Juniper configuration for PPP settings under the interface hierarchy, but this command specifically addresses the authentication method portion.', 'trans_template': 'set system authentication-order password'}]]": 1,
